<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monte Cavo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mons Albanus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Monte Cavo is the second-highest peak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alban-hills">Alban Hills</a> south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, rising to about 950 metres above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-albano">Lake Albano</a>. In antiquity, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> Albanus, it carried the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jupiter-latiaris">Jupiter Latiaris</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> peoples and later the Roman consuls celebrated the annual Latin festival. Roman tradition placed Alba Longa on the slopes nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monte-cavo.jpg" alt="Monte Cavo" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NikonZ7II · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monte_Cavo_Visto_da_castel_gandolfo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
